// Negative: Emergency entry attempted with duplicate emAdmins (not possible in Alloy set semantics, but for traceability, check for non-distinctness)
pred Scenario_EmergencyEntryWithDuplicateAdmin {
    some s: State |
        s.mode = Emergency and prevModeIsNormal[s] and #s.emAdmins = 2 and
        (some a: s.emAdmins | (sum b: s.emAdmins | b=a) > 1) // always false in Alloy sets, but included for completeness
} ERROR MESSAGE: .
This must be an integer expression.
Instead, it has the following possible type(s):
{PrimitiveBoolean}

Thought for a second
The error is here:

sum b: s.emAdmins | b = a
In Alloy, sum expects the expression after | to be an integer expression. But:

b = a
is a Boolean formula, not an integer. Alloy does not treat true as 1 and false as 0.

So this expression is invalid:

(sum b: s.emAdmins | b = a) > 1
Also, your comment is right: duplicates are impossible in Alloy sets. If s.emAdmins is a set, the same atom cannot appear twice. Therefore, “duplicate admin in s.emAdmins” cannot be modeled this way.

You should remove that condition. The scenario can just be written as:

pred Scenario_EmergencyEntryWithDuplicateAdmin {
  some s: State |
    s.mode = Emergency and
    prevModeIsNormal[s] and
    #s.emAdmins = 2
}
But that no longer checks for duplicates; it only says there are two emergency admins.

If your goal is a negative assertion proving duplicates are impossible, use something like:

assert NoDuplicateEmergencyAdmins {
  all s: State | #s.emAdmins = #s.emAdmins
}
That assertion is tautological, though, because set uniqueness is built into Alloy.

A more meaningful traceability comment would be:

// Not modeled as a scenario: duplicate admins cannot occur because emAdmins is a set.
If you really need to model duplicate selections, use a relation with positions or slots instead of a set, for example:

one sig Slot1, Slot2 {}

sig State {
  emAdminSelection: Slot -> lone Administrator
}

abstract sig Slot {}
Then duplicate selection can be expressed as:

pred Scenario_EmergencyEntryWithDuplicateAdmin {
  some s: State |
    s.mode = Emergency and
    prevModeIsNormal[s] and
    some a: Administrator |
      s.emAdminSelection[Slot1] = a and
      s.emAdminSelection[Slot2] = a
}
So the immediate fix is: do not use sum over a Boolean expression. The deeper fix is: sets cannot contain duplicates, so duplicate membership is not representable unless you model selections with slots or another non-set structure.


